<section class="three-tool-section" id="qurbani-shares"><h3>How many Qurbani shares are in each animal?</h3>
<p>The customary division is a single share for a sheep or goat, or a maximum of seven for a cow, buffalo or camel. This is discussed in <a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:1318a" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih Muslim 1318a</a>, a hadith relating a seven-person sacrificial arrangement. Its direct setting was Hady associated with the Prophet's lifetime; established legal discussion extends share rules to the Eid Udhiyah sacrifice.</p>
<p>See the animal-by-animal summary from <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/qurbani/qurbani-animals/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK</a>. An animal's size and price do not override eligibility requirements. The application cannot verify its age, health or the slaughter process, and cannot turn a financially divided sheep into seven religious shares.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="qurbani-modes"><h3>One-seventh shares versus funding the whole animal</h3>
<p>These are different questions. <strong>Share reservation:</strong> if a provider quotes a whole cow at a particular price, each of its seven equal religious shares corresponds to approximately one-seventh of that price. Reserving three shares means your planned share cost covers three-sevenths, with four shares still to be arranged.</p>
<p><strong>Whole-animal financing:</strong> if three friends agree to fund the entire cow, each friend may pay one-third of the price. But the <em>payment division</em> is not automatically the <em>religious attribution of seven shares</em>. Who intends which shares, whether some are additional voluntary sacrifices, and whether all participants meet the necessary conditions require separate confirmation with the provider and a qualified scholar.</p>
<p>The calculator uses minor-unit arithmetic (pence, cents or equivalent) to distribute rounding differences without losing or inventing money. This is a financial allocation, not an animal-price quotation or an actual booking.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="qurbani-example"><h3>Worked Qurbani example: three of seven shares in a cow</h3>
<div class="three-tool-example"><p>Assume a <strong>hypothetical total price of £700</strong> for one cow. Dividing into seven sacrificial shares yields <strong>£100 per share</strong>. Three shares would be <strong>£300</strong>, while the remaining four shares represent <strong>£400</strong>. This is the result of the calculator's <strong>reserve shares</strong> mode.</p><p>In the <strong>fund whole animal</strong> mode, three people contributing equally to the entire £700 invoice would instead contribute around £233.33 each, with one person's contribution adjusted by a penny so that the sum is exactly £700. This does not by itself establish which religious share each person owns.</p></div>
<p>Different providers price individual shares differently or bundle administrative, distribution and slaughter fees. Enter the actual quoted total for the arrangement you are assessing, and check precisely what the provider means by a “Qurbani share.”</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="qurbani-validity"><h3>Animal age, welfare, intentions and eligibility</h3>
<p>Age and fitness matter: common guidance lists roughly two years for cattle and five for camels, and at least one year for goats, with some differences or exceptions for sheep. Animals must be healthy and suitable, and humane slaughter must follow the accepted rules. <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/qurbani/qurbani-rules/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK's Qurbani rules</a> gives practical standards.</p>
<p>In the Hanafi school, Udhiyah has particular obligatory conditions for financially able residents; other schools commonly treat it as a highly recommended Sunnah. The exact position of family members, representatives, mixed intentions and shares donated on behalf of another person needs a knowledgeable adviser. A successful cost calculation proves none of these conditions.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="qurbani-timing"><h3>When does Qurbani begin and end?</h3>
<p>Udhiyah begins after the qualifying Eid al-Adha prayer on <strong>10 Dhul Hijjah</strong>. There is a scholarly difference about whether the final permitted day is 12 or 13 Dhul Hijjah. Your provider should follow a recognised local view, verify the Eid announcement, and schedule slaughter accordingly.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/tools/dhul-hijjah-planner/">Dhul Hijjah Planner</a> for <em>calculated</em> dates only. It cannot certify local moon-sighting decisions or supplier slaughter timing. Giving earlier to a charity can be an agency arrangement for a later valid sacrifice; confirm the organisation's process.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="qurbani-distribution"><h3>How Qurbani meat is shared and distributed</h3>
<p>A commonly recommended division is to use one part personally, give one to relatives or friends, and donate one to those in need; charities may distribute all meat to beneficiaries. This practical pattern is distinct from the <em>seven shares in a live animal</em>. One-third meat distribution and one-seventh sacrificial participation are not interchangeable measures.</p>
<p>See the <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/qurbani/distribute-qurbani-meat/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK meat-distribution discussion</a>. The app does not calculate a guaranteed meat yield from a quoted price because animal condition, weight, processing and charitable distribution vary.</p></section>
